"""
What a module entrypoint hands the module's code with each call: the local
clients declared for the module, and nothing else.

The module's code runs in an exec that the engine does not make the module, so
its own current workspace is its container, and a local client cannot resolve
there. The engine hands the entrypoint each declared local client
(currentModule.localClients) as a module source over the files it loaded for
that client: enough to serve it, and no way back to any workspace. A module in
the caller's workspace gets the clients the caller's config declares on its
scope; a module from git or a directory gets the ones its own config declares.

A git client needs no handover: the code serves it through serveModule.

The shared entrypoint and every static one carry this file as it is, so the
two forms hand over the same thing.

The list is asked of the engine at every call, never baked into a static
entrypoint at generation: a baked list is the module author's claim, while
for a module in the caller's workspace only the caller's config says which of
the caller's files the module may have. Do not simplify it into one.
"""
type ClientHandover {
  """
  Not read: an entrypoint written against the handover that resolved clients
  in the workspace keeps calling it the same way.
  """
  let workspace: Workspace!

  new(workspace: Workspace!) {
    self.workspace = workspace
    self
  }

  """
  One entry per declared local client: the module name its descriptor holds,
  and the ID of the module source the engine handed over for it.
  """
  pub clients: [HandedClient!]! {
    currentModule.localClients.{{moduleName, id}}.map { src =>
      HandedClient(name: src.moduleName, source: (src.id :: String!))
    }
  }
}

type HandedClient {
  pub name: String!
  pub source: String!

  new(name: String!, source: String!) {
    self.name = name
    self.source = source
    self
  }
}
